---
import DocsLayout from '../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import { Code } from 'astro:components';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');

const storeExample = `// Store-centric: state is gathered behind one feature boundary
type State = {
  count: number;
  user: User | null;
};

const store = createStore<State>({
  count: 0,
  user: null,
});

const doubled = store.select((state) => state.count * 2);`;

const atomicExample = `// Atomic / Graph-centric: state is split into composable nodes
const countAtom = atom(0);
const userAtom = atom<User | null>(null);

const doubledAtom = atom((get) => {
  return get(countAtom) * 2;
});`;

const secondaryAxisCode = `// Value-push: the notification carries the next value
box.subscribe((nextValue) => {
  render(nextValue);
});

// Invalidation + read: notification only says "changed"
box.subscribe(() => {
  const nextValue = box.getSnapshot();
  render(nextValue);
});

// Lazy graph: source writes mark derived values dirty
count.set(1);
doubled.read(); // recompute on demand if dirty`;
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Store vs Atomic"
  description="Understand the primary state management split between Store-centric architectures and Atomic / Graph-centric dependency node systems."
  currentPath={`${base}/store-vs-atomic/`}
>
  <h1>Store-centric vs Atomic / Graph-centric</h1>
  <p class="text-lg text-muted-foreground !mt-2">
    The primary design split is where state is organized: around feature stores, or around small composable nodes.
  </p>

  <hr />

  <h2>Store-centric</h2>

  <p>Store-centric systems organize state behind a feature or application boundary. A store, reducer, ViewModel, or BLoC owns a coherent slice of state and exposes actions, selectors, or derived values around that boundary.</p>

  <p><strong>Representatives:</strong> Redux, Zustand, TCA, Verge, Provider, BLoC, ViewModel + StateFlow</p>

  <p>The mental model: <em>"This feature owns this state shape."</em></p>

  <Code code={storeExample} lang="typescript" />

  <h3>Characteristics</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>State is grouped around features, screens, reducers, or view models</li>
    <li>Mutations are usually explicit: actions, commits, events, or setter functions</li>
    <li>Derived state is usually expressed with selectors, computed properties, pipelines, or <code>combine()</code></li>
    <li>The dependency structure is mostly designed by the developer</li>
  </ul>

  <hr />

  <h2>Atomic / Graph-centric</h2>

  <p>Atomic and graph-centric systems organize state as small units that compose into other units. The runtime captures dependencies while derived values are evaluated, then uses that graph to invalidate, rebuild, or notify only the affected readers.</p>

  <p><strong>Representatives:</strong> Jotai, Atoms, Riverpod, swift-state-graph, ReactiveState-Kotlin, Compose <code>derivedStateOf</code>, Signals, TanStack Store</p>

  <p>The mental model: <em>"This value depends on those values."</em></p>

  <Code code={atomicExample} lang="typescript" />

  <h3>Characteristics</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>State is split into atoms, providers, signals, nodes, or derived stores</li>
    <li>Derived state is a first-class primitive, not only a selector at the edge</li>
    <li>Dependencies are discovered from reads such as <code>get()</code>, <code>watch()</code>, <code>ref.watch()</code>, <code>.state</code>, or Snapshot reads</li>
    <li>The runtime owns more of the dependency graph and invalidation behavior</li>
  </ul>

  <hr />

  <h2>The Second Axis: Value Push vs Invalidation + Read</h2>

  <p><a href={`${base}/push-vs-pull/`}>Push and pull</a> are still useful, but they describe a different axis from Store vs Atomic. The question is not whether the state is stored in one feature boundary or many nodes. The question is: <strong>does the change notification carry the next value, or does it only invalidate and let consumers read later?</strong></p>

  <Code code={secondaryAxisCode} lang="typescript" />

  <p>This creates a matrix. A store-centric library can be value-pushing or invalidation/read. An atomic graph can also push updated values to mounted listeners, or it can push only dirty marks and recompute when read. Many modern systems are hybrids.</p>

  <div class="not-prose my-6 w-full overflow-auto rounded-lg border border-border bg-card">
    <table class="w-full min-w-[760px] caption-bottom text-sm">
      <thead class="[&_tr]:border-b">
        <tr class="border-b transition-colors">
          <th class="h-10 px-4 text-left align-middle font-medium text-muted-foreground">Organization</th>
          <th class="h-10 px-4 text-left align-middle font-medium text-muted-foreground">Value-push</th>
          <th class="h-10 px-4 text-left align-middle font-medium text-muted-foreground">Invalidation + read</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody class="[&_tr:last-child]:border-0">
        <tr class="border-b transition-colors hover:bg-muted/50">
          <td class="p-4 align-middle font-medium text-foreground">Store-centric</td>
          <td class="p-4 align-middle text-muted-foreground">Verge derived values, Zustand subscriptions, StateFlow, BLoC / Cubit</td>
          <td class="p-4 align-middle text-muted-foreground">Redux store subscriptions, TCA with Observation, Provider / ChangeNotifier</td>
        </tr>
        <tr class="transition-colors hover:bg-muted/50">
          <td class="p-4 align-middle font-medium text-foreground">Atomic / Graph-centric</td>
          <td class="p-4 align-middle text-muted-foreground">Riverpod, Atoms, ReactiveState-Kotlin</td>
          <td class="p-4 align-middle text-muted-foreground">swift-state-graph, Compose <code>derivedStateOf</code>, Jotai core, TanStack Store derived values</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>The labels are about the dominant mechanism, not a purity test. For example, signal-style systems often push invalidation through the graph, then pull the cached value on read.</p>
</DocsLayout>
